.TH NPASS 1 "2026" "npass 1.0" "npass manual"
.SH NAME
npass \- Wayland\-native password store with identity\-based name resolution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B npass
.I COMMAND
.I ID
.RI [ DIR/PASS " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B npass
is a rewrite of
.BR pass (1)
built around two decisions that change its on\-disk shape and its
day\-to\-day command line, compared to the standard tool.
.PP
First, every operation requires an
.IR ID :
an
.IR identity ,
which is a directory under
.B $NPASS_STORE
containing its own
.B .gpg\-id
and its own encrypted lookup map. There is no "bare path" mode and no
implicit root store \- a lone
.I DIR/PASS
argument is never resolved on its own. This is a deliberate removal,
not an omission: identities give a natural, memorable unit
(\c
.IR personal ", " work ", " client\-acme )
instead of one long, hard\-to\-recall path, and they let each group of
secrets have its own GPG recipient set.
.PP
Second, within an identity, a secret's
.I logical path
(what you type, e.g.
.IR email/gmail )
is never the name of a file on disk. Each identity keeps a
.B .map.gpg
file: an encrypted table binding logical paths to opaque,
randomly\-named ciphertext blobs under
.BR blobs/ .
Renaming or moving a secret within one identity only edits this map \-
the ciphertext blob and its mtime never change. Moving a secret
.I between
identities is the one operation that must decrypt and re\-encrypt,
because the recipient set changes. This means anyone who can list your
store's files (a backup tool, a synced folder, a compromised git
remote) sees only random readable filenames (pseudonyms such as Bavodu.gpg), never your service names.
.PP
.B npass
is Wayland\-only for clipboard operations: it uses
.BR wl\-copy (1)
with
.B \-\-sensitive
and
.BR \-\-paste\-once ,
and refuses outright if no Wayland session is active. There is no X11
fallback, by design.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "init " ID " " RECIPIENT ...
Create a new identity, encrypting its map to the given GPG recipients
(key IDs, fingerprints, or email addresses \- anything
.BR gpg (1)
accepts after \c
.BR \-r ).
.TP
.BI "init \-\-sign" "[=" KEYID "] " ID " " RECIPIENT ...
Same, additionally writing a detached signature
.RB ( .gpg\-id.sig )
over the new
.BR .gpg\-id .
See
.B SECURITY
for exactly what this does and does not protect against.
.TP
.BI "sign " ID " [" KEYID ]
(Re)sign an existing identity's
.BR .gpg\-id ,
e.g. after rotating its recipients, or to opt an identity created
before this feature existed into verification.
.TP
.BI "show " ID " " DIR/PASS
Decrypt and print a secret.
.TP
.BI "identities"
List the identities and how many secrets each holds
.RI ( "NAME \- N senhas" ).
The count is the number of blob files on disk; nothing is decrypted.
Alias:
.BR ids .
.TP
.BI "clip " "[FIELD] " ID " " DIR/PASS
Decrypt a secret and copy part of it to the Wayland clipboard. With two
arguments (ID and path) the password (first line) is copied. With three,
the first is
.IR FIELD :
.B pass
(the password),
.B all
(the whole entry), or the key of a
.I key: value
line in the entry, matched case\-insensitively, exact key first, then a
unique prefix. Cleared after
.B $NPASS_CLIP_TIME
seconds (default 45) or on first paste, whichever comes first.
.TP
.BI "insert " "[\-f] " ID " " DIR/PASS
Prompt twice (no echo) for a new secret and store it. With an existing
entry, prompts to overwrite unless
.B \-f
is given.
.TP
.BI "generate " "[\-n] [\-c] [\-f] [\-\-in\-place] " ID " " DIR/PASS " [" LENGTH ]
Generate a random password (default length: 25).
.B \-n
drops symbols (alphanumeric only).
.B \-c
copies it to the clipboard instead of printing it.
.B \-\-in\-place
replaces only the first line of an existing multi\-line entry,
preserving any following lines (notably an OTP URI) untouched.
.TP
.BI "edit " ID " " DIR/PASS
Open the entry's raw content in
.BR $EDITOR " (default " vi )
and save it back if changed.
.TP
.BI "rm " "[\-f] " ID " " DIR/PASS
Remove an entry: deletes its map row and shreds its ciphertext blob.
.TP
.BI "mv " ID " " DIR/PASS " " DIR2/PASS2
Rename within the same identity: a map\-only edit, the ciphertext blob
is untouched.
.TP
.BI "mv " ID " " DIR/PASS " " ID2 " " DIR2/PASS2
Move to a different identity: decrypts, re\-encrypts to the
destination identity's recipients, and removes the source entry.
.TP
.BI "ls " ID " [" DIR ]
List logical paths in an identity, optionally filtered to those under
a given prefix.
.TP
.BI "find " ID " " PATTERN
List logical paths whose name matches
.I PATTERN
(case\-insensitive substring/regex via
.BR grep (1)).
.TP
.BI "grep " ID " [" GREP\-OPTIONS "] " PATTERN
Decrypt every entry in the identity and search its content for
.IR PATTERN .
Inherently O(n) in the number of secrets \- there is no way to search
ciphertext without decrypting it.
.TP
.BI "update " "[opts] " ID " " PATTERN ...
Bulk password rotation. Each
.I PATTERN
is an exact logical path or a prefix ending in
.RI "\(dq/\(dq" ,
expanded against the identity's map. Options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-clip
copy the old password to the clipboard before rotating
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-no\-symbols
generate without symbols
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-length " N
generated password length
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-auto\-length
match the old password's length
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-provide
type the new password by hand instead of generating one
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-multiline
replace the entire entry content (read until EOF)
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-include " REGEX
only rotate entries whose old password matches
.I REGEX
.TP
.BI "\-e, \-\-exclude " REGEX
skip entries whose old password matches
.I REGEX
.TP
.B \-E, \-\-edit
open each matched entry in
.B $EDITOR
instead, ignoring every option above
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
skip the confirmation prompt
.RE
.TP
.BI "otp " ID " " DIR/PASS
Generate the current TOTP/HOTP code for an entry. For HOTP, also
increments and persists the stored counter. The OTP secret is never
passed as a command\-line argument to the code generator (see
.B SECURITY
below); if neither
.B otptool
nor a sufficiently recent
.B oathtool
is available,
.B npass
refuses rather than falling back to an unsafe method.
.TP
.BI "otp insert " "[\-f] [\-s \-i " ISSUER " \-a " ACCOUNT "] " ID " " DIR/PASS
Prompt for an
.B otpauth://
URI (or, with
.BR \-s ,
just the base32 secret plus issuer/account) and store it alongside any
existing password in the same entry.
.TP
.BI "otp uri " "[\-c|\-q] " ID " " DIR/PASS
Show the stored OTP URI, copy it to the clipboard, or render it as a
QR code in the terminal via
.BR qrencode (1)
(no image viewer, no display server needed).
.TP
.BI "otp clip " ID " " DIR/PASS
Generate the current code and copy it to the clipboard.
.TP
.BI "otp validate " URI
Check whether a string is a well\-formed
.B otpauth://
URI.
.TP
.BI "migrate " "[\-f] [\-\-delete\-source] " ID " " OLD_DIR
Import a legacy, vanilla
.BR pass (1)
store (a real directory tree where names are plaintext) into
.IR ID ,
obscuring every imported name behind the map. If
.I ID
does not exist yet and
.I OLD_DIR
has a
.BR .gpg\-id ,
the identity is created automatically with the same recipients, and
signed on the spot
.RB ( "as if by " "init \-\-sign" )
if
.I OLD_DIR
also has a
.BR .gpg\-id.sig .
Migrating into an
.I ID
that already exists never signs it as a side effect \- that stays an
explicit
.B sign
call.
.B \-\-delete\-source
shreds each old file after it is successfully imported (off by
default \- migration is non\-destructive unless asked otherwise).
.TP
.BI "migrate\-secrets " "[\-f] [\-\-delete\-source] " ID " " OLD_IDENTITY_DIR
Import an identity from the
.I pass\-secrets\-redesign
layout: a tree where both the category (directory) and the file are
already codenames, with a separate
.B .secrets.gpg
translating \c
.RI \(dq CODENAME " = Real Name" \(dq
per line, and optionally a
.B .mask.gpg
translating \c
.RI \(dq CATEGORY " = email\-alias" \(dq .
The codename (e.g.
.IR IST/Bvop )
becomes the npass logical path unchanged \- typing habits don't
change. The real name and email alias, when present, are appended as
plain note lines
.RI ( "# nome\-real: ..." ", " "# email\-alias: ..." )
inside the already\-encrypted blob itself, never in the map, in a git
commit, or on the command line: see
.B SECURITY
for why the codename, not the real name, had to stay the CLI\-visible
value. Auto\-creates and auto\-signs the destination identity under
the same rule as
.B migrate
above.
.TP
.BI "git " ARGS ...
Passthrough to
.BI "git \-C " $NPASS_STORE " " ARGS .
Run
.B npass git init
once to opt in to automatic commits; see
.B SECURITY
for exactly what those commits do and do not contain.
.TP
.BI "extension sign " PATH " [" KEYID ]
Write a detached signature
.RI ( PATH .sig)
over an extension executable with your own key. Required before that
extension will run \- see
.B EXTENSIONS
below.
.TP
.B "extension list"
List every
.B npass\-*
file in the extensions directory with its status: \c
.BR ok " if it would run right now, or " "recusada (" REASON )
naming the specific gate that failed. Checks only \- never executes
anything.
.TP
.B help
Show a command summary.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NPASS_ENABLE_EXTENSIONS
Must be exactly
.B 1
for
.BI "npass " NAME
to look for an
.BI npass\- NAME
extension when
.I NAME
isn't a built\-in command. Unset or any other value: extensions are
never even looked up, and an unrecognized command is always just an
error. Default: unset (off).
.TP
.B NPASS_EXTENSIONS_DIR
Directory
.B npass
looks in for
.B npass\-*
extension executables. Default:
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME/npass/extensions ", or " ~/.local/share/npass/extensions .
Deliberately not a
.B $PATH
scan \- see
.B EXTENSIONS
below.
.TP
.B NPASS_STORE
Root of the store. Default:
.B ~/.npass
(per user; the program itself is installed system\-wide).
.TP
.B NPASS_GPG
The
.B gpg
binary to invoke. Default:
.BR gpg .
.TP
.B NPASS_LANG
Selects the message language for runtime feedback (errors, warnings,
success confirmations, prompts). A value starting with
.B en
selects English; anything else, including unset, selects Portuguese
(the tool's default/core language). Command usage text and this man
page are not affected by this variable.
.TP
.B NPASS_CLIP_TIME
Seconds before a clipboard copy is cleared. Default: 45.
.TP
.B NPASS_GENERATED_LENGTH
Default length for
.B generate
and for
.B update
\&'s generated passwords when
.B \-l
is not given. Default: 25.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Used by
.B edit
and by
.BR "update \-E" .
Default:
.BR vi .
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Must be set (i.e. a Wayland session must be active) for
.B clip
and
.B otp clip
to work.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $NPASS_STORE/ID/.gpg\-id
One GPG recipient per line for identity
.IR ID .
.TP
.I $NPASS_STORE/ID/.map.gpg
Encrypted TSV: logical path, tab, opaque blob name. Rewritten
atomically (temp file + rename) under an
.BR flock (1)
held on
.IR .map.lock ,
so two concurrent writers to the same identity cannot silently drop
each other's entry.
.TP
.I $NPASS_STORE/ID/blobs/*.gpg
Individual encrypted secrets, named with random pronounceable pseudonyms. The
filename carries no information about what it contains.
.SH EXTENSIONS
.B npass
supports third\-party commands as external executables, off by
default. This is Model A (a separate process, any language) rather
than the sourced\-bash\-file model upstream
.BR pass (1)
actually uses \- a deliberate choice made when this was designed, not
a misunderstanding of what
.B pass
does: it trades direct access to npass's internal functions for a
much smaller, more auditable trust boundary (one directory, one
subprocess, checked fresh on every invocation).
.PP
.BI "npass " NAME " " ARGS ...
that don't match a built\-in command:
.IP 1. 4
does nothing unless
.B NPASS_ENABLE_EXTENSIONS=1
is set \- with it unset, this whole mechanism doesn't exist as far as
.B npass
is concerned, and an unknown command is always just an error;
.IP 2. 4
looks for an executable named
.BI npass\- NAME
in
.B $NPASS_EXTENSIONS_DIR
(never on
.BR $PATH ,
so the trust boundary is exactly one directory you control, not every
directory on your
.BR $PATH );
.IP 3. 4
if found, refuses to run it unless it passes every one of:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
regular file, not a symlink;
.IP \(bu 2
not group\- or other\-writable;
.IP \(bu 2
a detached signature
.RI ( NAME .sig,
made with
.BR "npass extension sign" )
exists and cryptographically verifies against the exact file;
.IP \(bu 2
.B and the signing key's fingerprint is among your OWN secret keys
.RB ( "gpg \-\-list\-secret\-keys" ),
not merely a key gpg can verify against.
.RE
.PP
That last gate is the actual point, confirmed against a real forged
case while this was built: an attacker with write access to the
extensions directory (a compromised sync target, a shared machine, a
malicious package) can plant a file and sign it with their own key \-
the signature verifies cryptographically just fine, gate 3 passes
outright. Gate 4 is what stops it, because it doesn't ask "is this
signature valid", it asks "did I sign this" \- unless the attacker
also holds the private key behind one of the fingerprints already in
your own secret keyring (meaning they've already stolen a key of
yours, a far higher bar than write access to one directory), the
extension is refused. A signature made by a key you've only ever
imported the
.I public
half of \- exactly what you'd hold for someone else's key, including
an attacker's, if you'd ever received anything signed by them \- fails
this gate even though
.BR gpg (1)
reports the signature itself as good.
.PP
Any other failure (missing signature, tampered file, unsafe
permissions, a symlink) is loud, not a silent fall\-through to
"unknown command": a file existing in the extensions directory at all
means something is really there, and pretending otherwise would hide
a real tampering attempt instead of surfacing it.
.PP
On success, npass
.BR exec (1)
\&'s the extension directly (no wrapper process) with
.BR NPASS_STORE ", " NPASS_GPG ", and " NPASS_LANG
exported, and the extension's exit code becomes npass's own.
.SH SECURITY
.TP
.B Commit messages never contain a logical path.
When git integration is enabled
.RB ( "npass git init" ),
every mutating command commits automatically with a message of the
exact form \(dq\c
.IR ACTION : " ID" \c
\(dq (e.g. \(dqinsert: personal\(dq), where
.I ACTION
is a fixed generic verb. The secret's logical path
.RI ( "email/gmail-personal-account" ,
say) never appears in a commit message. This matters because upstream
.BR pass (1)
writes the plaintext pass\-name directly into its commit messages,
which defeats any name obscuring the moment someone runs
.BR "git log" .
The git tree itself will still show which opaque blob filenames
changed under which identity directory \- that is unavoidable and
carries no information, since blob names are random.
.TP
.B The OTP secret never touches a process's command\-line arguments.
Command\-line arguments are visible to every user on the system via
.IR /proc/*/cmdline " or " ps .
.B npass
always pipes the OTP secret to the code generator over stdin. If
.B otptool
is unavailable and the installed
.B oathtool
is too old to read a base32 secret from stdin,
.B npass
refuses to generate a code rather than falling back to passing the
secret as an argument.
.TP
.B Clipboard entries are marked sensitive and single\-use where supported.
.B clip
and
.B otp clip
invoke
.B wl\-copy
with
.B \-\-sensitive
(a hint to clipboard managers such as
.B cliphist
not to persist the entry to disk) and
.B \-\-paste\-once
(served exactly once, then cleared). A background timeout
.RB ( $NPASS_CLIP_TIME )
additionally clears the clipboard for compositors or managers that
ignore both hints, but only if the clipboard still holds exactly what
was copied.
.TP
.B The map is never written non\-atomically.
Every map mutation is: decrypt current map, apply the change in
memory, encrypt to a temp file, then
.B mv
that temp file over
.IR .map.gpg ,
all while holding an exclusive
.BR flock (1)
on a separate lock file. A crash or a concurrent writer can therefore
never leave the map partially written or silently overwritten.
.TP
.B Signed identities catch silent recipient tampering on WRITE, not on read.
An identity created with
.B init \-\-sign
(or opted in later via
.BR sign )
gets a detached signature over its
.BR .gpg\-id .
Every subsequent write \- insert, generate, edit, otp insert, an
update rotation, a migration \- verifies that signature first and
refuses outright if it's missing content or doesn't verify. This
matters because
.B npass
never signs by default: an attacker with only write access to the
store (a compromised sync target, a malicious git remote, a shared
backup you don't fully trust) could otherwise replace
.B .gpg\-id
with their own key, and every secret you write from then on would
silently be encrypted to them too. Decrypting an existing secret
.RB ( show ", " clip ", " grep )
never reads
.B .gpg\-id
at all \- it uses your private key regardless of what the file
claims \- so tampering with it cannot expose secrets you already
have; it can only try to hijack secrets you haven't written yet, and
that is exactly the attempt this catches.
.TP
.B migrate\-secrets keeps the codename as the logical path on purpose.
The npass logical path is passed as a literal command\-line argument
on every
.BR show / edit / generate " etc. call \- it lands in shell history and"
.IR /proc/*/cmdline ,
and could be correlated against git commit timestamps to infer which
service a given commit touched even though the commit message itself
never names it (see the point above on commit messages). Using the
real service name as the logical path during migration would reopen
exactly that leak one layer up; keeping the codename closes it, at
the cost of the real name only being visible after a secret is
already decrypted.
.SH LIMITATIONS
There is no "classic" bare\-path mode: an
.I ID
is always required, even for a single, ungrouped secret \- you still
need at least one identity to hold it. This is intentional (see
.BR DESCRIPTION ),
not a missing feature.
.PP
.B find
and
.B grep
are O(n) in the number of entries in an identity, since a logical
path's name doesn't reveal which physical file to search and content
search requires decrypting every candidate. This matches the
inherent cost of searching an encrypted store and is not specific to
.BR npass .
.PP
.B migrate
only understands a vanilla
.BR pass (1)
directory\-tree layout;
.B migrate\-secrets
only understands the specific pass\-secrets\-redesign layout described
above. A store from any other name\-obscuring scheme needs its own
dedicated importer.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
npass init personal 1B95\&...940B7AE4E
npass insert personal email/gmail
npass otp insert \-f personal email/gmail
npass show personal email/gmail
npass clip personal email/gmail
npass otp personal email/gmail
npass update \-a \-f personal email/
npass git init
npass migrate personal ~/.password\-store
npass init \-\-sign personal 1B95\&...940B7AE4E
npass migrate\-secrets personal ~/old\-store/ID\-Pessoal/HG
npass extension sign ~/.local/share/npass/extensions/npass\-mycmd
NPASS_ENABLE_EXTENSIONS=1 npass mycmd
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pass (1),
.BR gpg (1),
.BR wl\-copy (1),
.BR oathtool (1),
.BR qrencode (1)
